<td id="S3.T1.6.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.6.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">FintextQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite></th>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">75<span id="S3.T1.6.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">*</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.6.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.6.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KnowMT-Bench (ours)</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">2.98</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">95.85</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">✓</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">✓</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">✓</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Question Sequence Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mirror real conversational patterns, we analyze the ShareGPT-Chinese-English-90k dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">shareAI, 2023</a>)</cite> and find the following distribution for knowledge-intensive dialogues up to five turns: 2-turn (38.5%), 3-turn (38.2%), 4-turn (14.0%), and 5-turn (5.4%). Since dialogues longer than 5 turns occur at a negligible rate (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 5\%</annotation></semantics></math>), we merge them into 5 turns and set the maximum dialogue length to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\max}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\max}=5</annotation></semantics></math>. In our benchmark, dialogue lengths are drawn from this empirical distribution of 2–5 turns (37.45%, 37.45%, 14.98%, and 10.11%, respectively).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each single-turn question <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate a multi-turn question sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}^{(d)}=(q^{(d)}_{1},\dots,q^{(d)}_{N_{d}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}^{(d)}=(q^{(d)}_{1},\dots,q^{(d)}_{N_{d}})</annotation></semantics></math> of a sampled length <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}\in\{2,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}\in\{2,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the final question <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{(d)}_{N_{d}}=q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{(d)}_{N_{d}}=q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The preceding questions <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q^{(d)}_{1:N_{d}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{(d)}_{1:N_{d}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are created under a human-in-the-loop paradigm where combining LLM-based generation with manual review ensured that each sequence adheres to three key principles: (1) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Progressive Context Building</span>: Questions gradually establish background or narrow the scope. (2) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intent Preservation</span>: The sequence naturally leads to the final question <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> without semantic drift. (3) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Answer Leakage</span>: Preceding questions do not reveal or hint at the answer to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. In total, the procedure produced 801 question sequences. We provide more details in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During evaluation, we evaluate models using a dynamic setting where the model self-generates its own dialogue history. Specifically, for a given question sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the history for the final turn, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{(d)}_{N_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{(d)}_{N_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is constructed by recursively generating each intermediate answer:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{(d)}_{t}=\mathcal{M}(q^{(d)}_{1},a^{(d)}_{1},\dots,q^{(d)}_{t-1},a^{(d)}_{t-1},q^{(d)}_{t}),\quad\text{for }t=1,\dots,N_{d}-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(d)}_{t}=\mathcal{M}(q^{(d)}_{1},a^{(d)}_{1},\dots,q^{(d)}_{t-1},a^{(d)}_{t-1},q^{(d)}_{t}),\quad\text{for }t=1,\dots,N_{d}-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Finally, the model generates the targeted answers <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(d)}_{N_{d}}=\mathcal{M}(H^{(d)}_{N_{d}},q^{(d)}_{N_{d}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(d)}_{N_{d}}=\mathcal{M}(H^{(d)}_{N_{d}},q^{(d)}_{N_{d}})</annotation></semantics></math> for MT-LFQA.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.21856v1/3_evaluation.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/169;" width="521" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Overview of the two-stage natural language inference-based evaluation. Here, <span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GTA</span> refers to <span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>round <span id="S3.F3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>ruth <span id="S3.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>nswer, and the detailed prompts for evaluation are provided in Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Prompts ‣ D.3 Additional Details for Automatic Evaluation ‣ D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Evaluation Framework</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically evaluate model performance in MT-LFQA, we introduce a comprehensive evaluation framework, which has two components: a two-stage automated pipeline to assess factual alignment, and a three-dimensional metric suite to quantify performance from the pipeline’s outputs. The evaluation focuses on the final-turn response (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) in a dialogue, measuring it against the ground-truth answer (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the ground-truth must-have facts set <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Two-Stage Evaluation Pipeline</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core of our framework is a two-stage, NLI-based evaluation pipeline, which is designed to assess factual consistency at a fine-grained level. In the first stage, we employ <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite> as a <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">decomposer</span> to break down long-form answers into minimal, self-contained factual units. For the ground-truth answers (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>), this process yields a set of atomic facts (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>), which subsequently undergoes manual verification and refinement to establish the gold standard. During testing, the model-generated answer (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) is dynamically decomposed into a corresponding set of atomic statements (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>). In the second stage, we use <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct</span> as the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NLI-based evaluator</span> to assess the factual consistency between ground-truth and generated answers. To avoid the quadratic computational complexity, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|\mathcal{S}_{j}|\cdot|\mathcal{F}_{j}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|\mathcal{S}_{j}|\cdot|\mathcal{F}_{j}|)</annotation></semantics></math>, of an exhaustive comparison between atomic statement sets, we adopt an efficient symmetric approach. Completeness is measured by judging each gold-standard fact <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> against the full model-generated answer <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, correctness is measured by judging each generated statement <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> against the full ground-truth answer <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The evaluator classifies each relationship as <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entailment</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contradiction</span>, or <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neutral</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the reliability of our pipeline, we conduct a human validation on a sample of 100 generated answers, randomly drawn from representative LLMs across single-turn or multi-turn settings. For the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">decomposition stage</span>, we compare the decomposer’s output against human decomposition on these 100 answers. The process demonstrates high fidelity, with a Symmetric Mean Absolute Percentage Error (SMAPE) of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">18.1%</span> in statement counts and an omission rate of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5.9%</span>. Errors mainly arose from under-segmentation rather than semantic distortion. For the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">judgment stage</span>, these dialogues were used to construct 1,687 evaluation NLI-pairs. The agreement between our NLI-based evaluator and the resulting gold annotations from majority voting among three annotators reached an F1-score of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">83.6%</span>, confirming that our pipeline provides a reliable measure of factual consistency. Further details on the human annotation process, including the models sampled and the prompts utilized, are available in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Additional Details for Automatic Evaluation ‣ D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Prompts ‣ D.3 Additional Details for Automatic Evaluation ‣ D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Three-Dimensional Metric Framework</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The NLI judgments are aggregated into a suite of metrics organized under three distinct dimensions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Factuality</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension quantifies the correctness and completeness of the must-have fact provided. It is based on <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Factual Precision</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the fraction of generated statements that are entailmented, and <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Factual Recall</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the fraction of ground-truth facts covered. These are combined into the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Factual F1</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>) score for a comprehensive assessment.</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R_{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{P_{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{j}^{+}|}{|\mathcal{S}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{S_{f}}=\frac{2\mathbf{P_{f}}\mathbf{R_{f}}}{\mathbf{P_{f}}+\mathbf{R_{f}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R_{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{P_{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{j}^{+}|}{|\mathcal{S}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{S_{f}}=\frac{2\mathbf{P_{f}}\mathbf{R_{f}}}{\mathbf{P_{f}}+\mathbf{R_{f}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reliability (Factual Hallucination)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension measures the extent of factual hallucination. Analogous to factuality, it is quantified using the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">False Claim Rate</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P_{fc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟𝐜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P_{fc}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the fraction of generated statements that are contradicted, and the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Misrepresentation Rate</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐦</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the fraction of ground-truth facts contradicted by the (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>). These are unified into the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucination F1</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>) score.</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R_{m}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{-}|}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{P_{fc}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{j}^{-}|}{|\mathcal{S}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{S_{h}}=\frac{2\mathbf{P_{fc}}\mathbf{R_{m}}}{\mathbf{P_{fc}}+\mathbf{R_{m}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐦</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟𝐜</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟𝐜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐦</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>𝐟𝐜</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>𝐦</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R_{m}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{-}|}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{P_{fc}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{j}^{-}|}{|\mathcal{S}_{j}|},\quad\mathbf{S_{h}}=\frac{2\mathbf{P_{fc}}\mathbf{R_{m}}}{\mathbf{P_{fc}}+\mathbf{R_{m}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Information Delivery Efficiency</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension assesses the utility of the model’s response by measuring the token cost of conveying information. This provides an intuitive measure of efficiency, as users directly interact with the token count. We report <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the average tokens per correctly entailed fact, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the average tokens per contradicted fact, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the average tokens per to cover the entire set of ground-truth facts. Lower values indicate higher efficiency.</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{T(a_{j})}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|},\quad\mathbf{D_{h}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{T(a_{j})}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{-}|},\quad\mathbf{D_{R}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{T(a_{j})}{r_{f}(j)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{T(a_{j})}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|},\quad\mathbf{D_{h}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{T(a_{j})}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{-}|},\quad\mathbf{D_{R}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}}\frac{T(a_{j})}{r_{f}(j)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is the token length of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{f}(j)=\tfrac{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{f}(j)=\tfrac{|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|}{|\mathcal{F}_{j}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is the factual recall for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the denominator of any term equals zero, i.e. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{+}|=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{-}|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{F}_{j}^{-}|=0</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{f}(j)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{f}(j)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, that term is estimated by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\limits_{k\in\mathcal{D}}\tfrac{T(a_{k})}{|\cdot|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\limits_{k\in\mathcal{D}}\tfrac{T(a_{k})}{|\cdot|}</annotation></semantics></math> of the corresponding metric.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21856v1/4_f1_vs_hall.svg" id="S3.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/450;" width="548" height="450"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Factuality Baseline</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21856v1/4_eff_vs_eff.svg" id="S3.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/450;" width="548" height="450"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Information Delivery Efficiency</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
Results for LLMs Performance on Factuality and Information Delivery Efficiency.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate a broad set of popular LLMs, including the DeepSeek series: DeepSeek-V3-0324, denoted as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-V3</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and DeepSeek-R1-0528, denoted as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-R1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, OpenAI’s GPT models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18, denoted as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o mini</span>, and gpt-4o-2024-08-06, denoted as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o</span>, Meta’s Llama family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, denoted as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.1-8B</span> and Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct, denoted as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.3-70B</span>, the Qwen family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite>: Qwen-2.5-7B/14B/32B/72B-Instruct, denoted collectively as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen-2.5-7B/14B/32B/72B</span>, as well as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QwQ-32B</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini-2.5-Pro</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Token counts are computed using tiktoken’s gpt-4o tokenizer.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/openai/tiktoken" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/openai/tiktoken</a></span></span></span> All experiments are conducted on NVIDIA A800 GPUs. See detailed experiment settings in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Detailed experiment setting ‣ D.3 Additional Details for Automatic Evaluation ‣ D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reveal the relations between different performance dimensions, we map all model performances into scatter plots (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Information Delivery Efficiency ‣ 3.3.2 Three-Dimensional Metric Framework ‣ 3.3 Evaluation Framework ‣ 3 KnowMT-Bench ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). We present four primary metrics (also used in the experiments that follow); detailed numerical results and additional metric values are provided in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Additional Experiments ‣ D.3 Additional Details for Automatic Evaluation ‣ D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>. A clear observation from the plots is a systematic shift in performance when moving from single-turn to multi-turn settings. Specifically, in Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ Information Delivery Efficiency ‣ 3.3.2 Three-Dimensional Metric Framework ‣ 3.3 Evaluation Framework ‣ 3 KnowMT-Bench ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>, most models exhibit a top-left shift from their single-turn to multi-turn counterparts, indicating a decrease in the Factual F1 score (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and an increase in the Hallucination F1 score (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Similarly, in Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ Information Delivery Efficiency ‣ 3.3.2 Three-Dimensional Metric Framework ‣ 3.3 Evaluation Framework ‣ 3 KnowMT-Bench ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>, the points generally shift to the top-right, which means that models require more tokens to convey correct facts (higher <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>), while their generated factual errors also become sparser (higher <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>). This pronounced degradation in factuality, coupled with the challenge of efficiently delivering correct information in multi-turn dialogues, highlights the unique challenges posed by the MT-LFQA task. Within this general trend, several other patterns are also apparent: proprietary models such as GPT-4o define the performance frontier, and larger models generally outperform smaller ones within the same family. However, it is noteworthy that models optimized for Chain-of-Thought (e.g., DeepSeek-R1 to DeepSeek-V3, QWQ-32B to Qwen-2.5-32B) show no advantage in fact capability, suggesting that current CoT mechanisms may not directly translate to more factual answers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21856v1/4_relative_diff_summary.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/119;" width="521" height="119"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Relative difference between multi-turn and single-turn across models. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, the relationships between data points also reveal two underlying correlations. First, Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ Information Delivery Efficiency ‣ 3.3.2 Three-Dimensional Metric Framework ‣ 3.3 Evaluation Framework ‣ 3 KnowMT-Bench ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> illustrates a moderate negative correlation between the Factual F1 score (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the Hallucination F1 score (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>) (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}=0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}=0.49</annotation></semantics></math>, t-test <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>), which suggests that as a model’s factuality improves, its tendency to factual hallucinate generally decreases. This points to a potential strategy for mitigating hallucinations: enhancing a model’s intrinsic knowledge may be an effective path to reducing false claims. Second, Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ Information Delivery Efficiency ‣ 3.3.2 Three-Dimensional Metric Framework ‣ 3.3 Evaluation Framework ‣ 3 KnowMT-Bench ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> reveals a strong positive correlation between the token cost per correct fact (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and per contradicted fact (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>) (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}=0.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}=0.82</annotation></semantics></math>, t-test <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). This indicates that most models tend to be uniformly concise or verbose, rather than dynamically adjusting their efficiency based on the correctness of the information. For instance, the GPT-4o family is characterized by low costs on both metrics, demonstrating high efficiency. An interesting outlier in the single-turn setting is Gemini-2.5-Pro, which deviates significantly from the regression line; its <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> is exceptionally high for its given <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>, successfully pushing this trade-off boundary. Notably, this desirable characteristic disappears in the multi-turn dialogue setting, where its performance aligns with the general trend. Therefore, a key direction for future research is to design models or strategies that can break this trade-off by achieving a low <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> while simultaneously increasing <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the multi-turn dialogue. A detailed holistic four-quadrant analysis is provided in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Four-Quadrant Analysis ‣ Appendix G Additional Experiments ‣ D.3 Additional Details for Automatic Evaluation ‣ D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21856v1/5_replace_metrics_bar_chart.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/119;" width="521" height="119"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Replace: the target model generates the final-turn answer while conditioning on GPT-4o’s dialogue history (last user query unchanged).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>The Source of Multi-turn Performance Degradation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main results demonstrate a clear performance degradation when models transition from single-turn LFQA to MT-LFQA. To deconstruct this phenomenon, we first analyze the influence of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dialogue length (i.e., the number of conversational turns)</span> by computing the relative change (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m</annotation></semantics></math>) of each metric from its single-turn baseline, defined as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m=(m_{\text{multi}}-m_{\text{single}})/m_{\text{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>multi</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>single</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>single</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m=(m_{\text{multi}}-m_{\text{single}})/m_{\text{single}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows a clear dichotomy across dimensions. Information delivery efficiency, quantified by <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{D_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{D_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{D_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{D_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, appears to be directly influenced by dialogue length; as the conversation lengthens, the token cost per fact steadily increases, indicating progressively lower efficiency. In contrast, factual capability (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>) do not exhibit a clear monotonic trend with the number of rounds. This suggests that the degradation in fact capability is not primarily driven by the dialogue length. Another observation is that despite the lack of a length-based trend, a universal performance drop occurs in all multi-turns compared to the single-turn baseline. We therefore hypothesize that the LLM-generated dialogue history itself acts as a form of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contextual noise</span> that specifically impairs fact capability, regardless of its dialogue length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To test this noise hypothesis, we devise a further experiment. As constructing a perfectly “noiseless” dialogue history is infeasible, we investigate the effect of introducing a <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weaker noise</span> context. We hypothesize that the history self-generated by weaker models constitutes ”stronger noise” due to its inherently lower fact capability, while history from a superior model represents ”weaker noise.” As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, conditioning the weaker models on this weaker noise context significantly improves their final-turn factuality (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>) while suppressing hallucinations (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>). These results, along with our initial analysis, support the hypothesis and lead to a clear conclusion: the performance degradation of fact capability in MT-LFQA is primarily attributable to the contextual noise from the model’s self-generated history, not the longer dialogue.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            A finding which underscores that our dynamic evaluation setting more faithfully captures real-world model performance</span></span></span> Therefore, a promising direction for mitigating this decline is to develop strategies that can effectively filter noise from the dialogue history while incorporating useful contextual information. For more results of interpretability experiments, see Appendix <a href="#A7.SS6" title="G.6 Interpretability Analysis ‣ Appendix G Additional Experiments ‣ D.3 Additional Details for Automatic Evaluation ‣ D.2 Additional Details for Multi-Turn Dialogue Generation ‣ Appendix D Annotation Details ‣ KnowMT-Bench: Benchmarking Knowledge-Intensive Long-Form Question Answering in Multi-Turn Dialogues" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Results across different domains. Blocks are ordered as <span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">non-domain</span> (base) followed by a consolidated <span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">domain-specific</span> block. Headers annotate the applicable domain-specific model: Finance (Fin-R1) and Medical (HuatuoGPT). <span id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Underlined</span> values denote improvements over the base model under the same domain and turn setting. White rows indicate single-turn results, while gray rows indicate multi-turn results.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:58.8pt;vertical-align:-27.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-132.3pt,19.6pt) scale(0.600283702612908,0.600283702612908) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T2.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finance</span></th>
<td id="S4.T2.8.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T2.8.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-finance</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.8.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T2.8.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medical</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.8.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T2.8.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-medical</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.8.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
